# Validation Strategy (no new dependencies)

## Goal

Validate inputs consistently without adding validation libraries.

## Where validation lives

- **Route handlers**: validate request shape at the boundary.
- **Services**: validate invariants when enforcing domain policies.
- **Utils**: keep small coercion/guard helpers under `utils/validation/`.

## Patterns

- Prefer `unknown` inputs at boundaries + explicit narrowing.
- Use tiny guard/coercion helpers instead of casting.
- If the project already uses Zod, prefer Zod schemas at boundaries; do not add Zod only for trivial validation.

## Recommended helpers (examples)

- `utils/validation/isRecord.ts` -> `value is Record<string, unknown>` (arrays are not records; exclude them)
- `utils/validation/coerceFirstString.ts` -> `unknown -> string | null` (takes the first entry when given a string array)
- `utils/validation/coerceNumber.ts` -> `unknown -> number | null`
- `utils/validation/isNonEmptyString.ts` -> `value is string`

Guards must be pure and declare a type predicate (`value is T`) so narrowing survives the call site.

## Zod (when the project already uses it)

- Prefer `.safeParse()` over `.parse()` at boundaries; branch on `result.success` instead of catching.
- Derive the type from the schema with `z.infer<typeof Schema>`; never hand-write a parallel interface.
- Keep schemas in `types/<area>/`, named `<Thing>Schema`, exported alongside their inferred type.
- Use `result.error.format()` to map issues onto field-level messages.

## Rules

- No inline types or helpers inside route handlers.
- If validation logic grows, split into dedicated helpers (one function per file).
